# winevulkan, opengl32, win32u: reduce CPU overhead in OpenGL and Vulkan apps
# https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/12024 state=closed
# This MR reduces CPU overhead when running Vulkan and OpenGL applications. Since I tested it on an overly powerful Intel Xeon E5-2699v4 server CPU and a weak graphics card. I would like to see results on Core 2 Duo and Core 2 Quad and first generations i3 i5 i7, as they are much weaker in ~15-20 times or other older CPUs — I hope there are such users here.

So, here’s the most interesting thing I found out as part research project

In OpenGL, perf-combined branch provides a measurable reduction in CPU system time by 5.8% (from 1.20s to 1.13s) use lock-free cache of WGL contexts in TEB and the skipping of unnecessary user_lock locks during frame presentation

In Vulkan, CPU time in userspace also decreased slightly (from 1.31s to 1.28s) use binary search in loader_thunks and stack buffer queue submission

Summary in percentage (Xeon E5-2699v4):
- -2.2% CPU time in openGL (reduction in kernel sys time by -5.8%) and -0.1% in Vulkan
- avg RSS +0.5% in OpenGL and -0.2% lower RAM consumption in Vulkan 

